# An address, once something is associated.
#
# depends-on wpa BECAUSE THERE IS NOTHING TO GET AN ADDRESS ON OTHERWISE. On a
# machine with ethernet this would be wrong -- dhcpcd should run regardless --
# but the test machine has no ethernet controller at all, and a service that
# waits for wifi on a machine with none would be a bug worth having rather than
# a silent misconfiguration.
#
# dhcpcd DAEMONISES ITSELF and dinit is told so. -B would keep it in the
# foreground; type = bgprocess with pid-file is the shape dinit documents for
# a process that backgrounds and leaves a pid behind.
#
# THE resolv.conf HOOK ALREADY WORKS and needs nothing here. Measured on the
# laptop: dhcpcd's 20-resolv hook writes /etc/resolv.conf with the nameservers
# the lease carried, because /etc is on the tmpfs overlay and is writable. An
# earlier draft of this round assumed DNS was broken and planned a fix for it;
# it was not broken.
# NO INTERFACE IS NAMED. An earlier draft said `dhcpcd wlan0`, which is what
# the test machine calls it and is not a fact about any other machine --
# wpa-start finds the interface by looking for /sys/class/net/*/wireless, and
# naming it here would have been the one place that disagreed. With no argument
# dhcpcd manages every interface it finds, which is also correct on a machine
# that has ethernet as well.
#
# type = scripted, NOT bgprocess. dhcpcd daemonises and writes a pid file per
# interface -- /run/dhcpcd/wlan0.pid -- so there is no single path dinit can
# watch when the interface name is not known in advance. Running it as a step
# that completes is accurate: `dhcpcd` without -B forks and returns.
type = scripted
command = /usr/sbin/dhcpcd
restart = false
depends-on = wpa
